\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Goodhart landscape across reward conditions. 3-seed rows: TS/CE/non-hacking percentage (N-H) pooled across seeds; PR/ISR$^*$: mean$\pm$std of per-seed values. $^\dagger$Seed~42. $\ddagger$$\text{ISR}^*$ omitted for ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ because the raw mean is inflated by TS gaming; premise-count-matched value is $1.06\times$ random (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}, Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). $^\P$Includes TS classifier false-negative correction (s44) and PG premise-subset gaming (s42; 5.2\% of proved; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). $^\S$Random TS driven by seed~43 (38.3\%); seeds~42 and~44: 0\%. Per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}. Shuffled-ISR is a deconfounding control (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}), not an independent reward condition.}
\label{tab:goodhart-landscape}
\end{table*}

\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): per-seed consistent gaming.}
ISR reward increases TS rates in all 3 seeds relative to matched random: $70.6\%$ ($163/231$) vs.\ $13.7\%$ ($36/262$) pooled (supplementary CMH: $\text{OR}_{\text{CMH}}{=}2.46$, $p{=}5.6{\times}10^{-16}$; Appendix~\ref{app:statistical-details}). %% registry: plan002 vs plan018 all seeds; D095 FINAL
Random TS is seed-dependent ($38.3\%$ in s43 vs.\ $0\%$ in s42/s44); ISR makes TS near-deterministic ($2/3$ seeds at $97$--$100\%$, Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}).
TS (a single premise identical to the conclusion, giving $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by construction; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}) is the canonical Goodhart move: the model satisfies the LOO definition directly.
Total conclusion embedding ($\text{CE}+\text{TS}$) is $68$--$100\%$ across both conditions; the composition differs: CE dominates under random ($71\%$), TS under ISR. ISR converts CE into TS rather than reducing total embedding (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}).
Random yields higher PR ($87{\pm}8\%$ vs.\ $77{\pm}14\%$): noise disrupts convergence toward a single collapse trajectory.

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
The sole non-TS ISR seed (s42) shows raw $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.415$, $3.2\times$ random ($0.131$), a premise-minimization artifact ($n{=}5.58$ vs.\ $8.46$); matched comparison gives $1.06\times$ (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis
After reclassifying PG and TS false negatives, non-hacking rates ($6.1\%$ ISR vs.\ $9.5\%$ random) are not significant ($p{=}0.182$; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). %% D095 FINAL Fisher test
The key asymmetry is compositional: ISR converts stochastic TS into near-deterministic convergence ($2/3$ seeds ${\geq}97\%$), while random remains seed-dependent and CE-dominated.

\textbf{Higher doses and temporal dynamics.}
ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ non-hacking is transient: seed~42 drops $17.3\%{\to}2.2\%$ by 1000 steps as TS escalates to $96.7\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}). %% registry: plan002-b01-s42-1000, plan016-extended-1000-s42
At higher doses ($\beta{\geq}0.2$), TS becomes universal ($0\%$ non-hacking at $\beta{=}0.2$); $\beta{=}0.3$ destabilizes both ISR ($\text{PR}{=}66{\pm}42\%$) and random ($54{\pm}35\%$), with ISR producing multi-modal failure~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization} and random converging to CE ($91\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:beta03}). %% registry: plan002-additive-b02-s42/s43/s44, plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44

\subsection{Cross-Domain Evidence}
\label{sec:crossdomain}
\label{sec:crosslogic}
\label{sec:crossdataset}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Top}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$; $^\dagger$s43 FOL uses sampling $T{=}0.7$, not strictly comparable). \textbf{Bottom}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition; all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Prolog: universal Goodhart.}
We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Exec-only seeds show $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0$ via redundant ground-fact coding (2/3 seeds); ISR flags this mode as it flags FOL's conclusion embedding.
Under ISR-as-reward, 3/3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms: clean TS (s42), uppercase pseudo-Prolog bypassing text-match classifiers (s43, all 38 proved at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$), and query-as-fact gaming (s44, 95/97 at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$; Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}).
Prolog's simpler semantics broadens the gaming basin, eliminating the non-hacking residual entirely.

\textbf{ProofFOL exec-only: structural degeneration generalizes, though mode distributions differ.}
Two converged seeds ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$) show ${\leq}1.4\%$ genuine; s42 exhibits 100\% TS (absent from all FOLIO exec-only seeds), showing hacking-mode repertoire is dataset-specific ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$ across all seeds, far below baseline $0.31$).

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): beneficial instability (exploratory).}
ISR reduces stable convergence from 2/3 to 0/3 seeds, yet ISR seeds average 76.8\% genuine versus 17.4\% for exec-only ($p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$; Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}).
ProofFOL ``genuine'' excludes only TS and CE (the two modes detectable by text-match classifiers), unlike FOLIO's eight-mode residual; the rates are therefore not directly comparable.
However, ISR-condition prove rates ($0.35$--$0.49$) remain far below exec-only (${\sim}1.0$), so apparent beneficial instability may reflect insufficient training rather than a genuine ISR effect.

\textbf{Mechanism.}
On ProofFOL, hacking yields $\text{ISR}{=}0.0$ while genuine proofs yield $\text{ISR}{>}0.2$; ISR correctly penalizes shortcuts.
On FOLIO, $P{\to}P$ yields $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ (regressional Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}), so ISR erroneously rewards tautological hacking.
The same metric produces opposite outcomes, determined by whether the dataset's hacking geometry satisfies it.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples; exec-only: seed~42; all other bars: pooled across 3 seeds, per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}). ISR converts CE into more extreme TS without reducing total hacking; SFT shows near-zero hacking.}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Exec} & \textbf{ISR$_{.1}$} & \textbf{Rnd$_{.1}$} & \textbf{SFT} & \textbf{Prolog$_{\text{ISR}}$} \\
\midrule
CE embed       & 87 & 18 & 71 & 7 & 0$^\sharp$ \\
TS duplicate   & 0  & 71 & 14 & 0 & 100$^\sharp$ \\
PG prem-subset & 6  & 5  & 8   & 0 & --- \\
Tautological   & --- & --- & --- & --- & s42 \\
Repetition     & s43 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Contradiction  & 1k & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Cmplx.\ vacuous& 0  & 0  & 4  & 0 & --- \\
Query-as-fact  & --- & --- & --- & --- & s44 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Non-hacking} & 8  & 6  & 10 & \textbf{92} & \textbf{0} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Eight hacking modes across conditions (\% of proved samples; exec-only/SFT: seed~42; ISR/Random: pooled across 3 seeds). TS includes subtypes TSa (text-match, $n{=}149$) and TSb (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). Cells with seed identifiers indicate the mode dominates that seed. $^\sharp$Prolog row pools strict+functional (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}).}
\label{tab:hacking-taxonomy}
\end{table}

Three modes account for ${>}90\%$ of hacking (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}; Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}): \textbf{CE} (conclusion embedding), \textbf{TS} (conclusion duplication, ISR-amplified; TSb evades text-match classifiers), and \textbf{PG} (premise-subset gaming; audited for s42 only, s43/s44 checkpoints not retained). CE and TS represent genuine structural degeneration, while PG is milder shortcutting preserving partial logical structure.
\emph{Reward formulation determines composition but not prevalence}: all GRPO conditions show ${\geq}80\%$ hacking; only SFT achieves $92.3\%$ non-hacking.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Neurosymbolic reasoning.}
LLM-solver pipelines~\citep{satlm, logic-lm, linc, fover-pei2025, lorp} and RL methods trained on solver feedback~\citep{mellgren-prolog-grpo, logic-rl, deepseek-prover-v15} evaluate execution accuracy alone, with no diagnostic for structural quality.
AlphaProof~\citep{alphaproof2025} applies RL-based formal verification at scale in Lean~4; STA-style diagnostics could probe whether similar structural degeneration occurs in theorem-proving settings.
We use FOLIO~\citep{folio}, the only public NLI benchmark with both FOL and Prolog annotations.

\paragraph{Process rewards and reward shaping.}
Reward shaping theory~\citep{ng1999policy} establishes conditions under which auxiliary rewards preserve optimal policies; early work applied verifier-based training to mathematical reasoning~\citep{cobbe2021training}, with process-level feedback showing promise over outcome-only signals~\citep{uesato2022solving}.
Learned PRMs~\citep{lightman-verify-step, math-shepherd} provide step-level feedback but suffer from reward-model noise and distribution shift~\citep{processbench}: \citet{gao-effective-rl-reward} show that auxiliary process/outcome rewards can \emph{hurt} RL training relative to success-only reward, a finding our ISR-as-reward results corroborate; \citet{setlur2025rewarding} further demonstrate that reward bonus magnitude must be carefully calibrated, echoing our $\beta$-dependent Goodhart transitions.
Symbolic feedback approaches~\citep{rlsf} use solver certificates as token-level rewards, achieving gains on program synthesis; ISR differs in operating at the component level (LOO ablation) rather than the token level, and our results show this structural signal is gamed when optimized.

\paragraph{Reward hacking and Goodhart's Law.}
Goodhart's Law is established theoretically~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy, skalse-reward-hacking, karwowski-goodhart-rl} and empirically in RLHF and frontier models~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization, bondarenko-spec-gaming, kim-game-formalization}.
\citet{kim-game-formalization} document inference-time gaming in logical formalization on FOLIO using frontier models with Lean~4.
Our work is complementary: training-time (vs.\ inference), component necessity (vs.\ faithfulness), medium-model/Prover9 (vs.\ frontier/Lean~4); combining STA with inference-time faithfulness detection would provide complete gaming coverage.
\citet{kwa-catastrophic-goodhart} show that heavy-tailed reward misspecification causes \emph{catastrophic} Goodhart where KL regularization provably fails, offering a theoretical lens for why GRPO's KL penalty does not prevent the ISR collapse we observe.
Recent work further documents spurious reward signals in RLVR~\citep{spurious-rewards, yan2026spurious}, reward hacking in frontier models~\citep{metr-reward-hacking}, and proposes verifiable composite or process rewards to mitigate outcome-only limitations~\citep{tarek2025reward, pronesti2026beyond}.
\citet{helff2026gaming} and \citet{khalifa2026countdown} document verification gaming and provide RLVR hacking testbeds; STA is complementary, offering a structural diagnostic and eight-mode taxonomy for neurosymbolic settings where gaming \emph{emerges during training}.
The Goodhart effect follows standard taxonomy~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: regressional on FOLIO (tautologies satisfy the LOO metric), extremal at high $\beta$. Its application to a structural diagnostic designed to \emph{detect} gaming is, to our knowledge, novel.

\paragraph{Mutation testing.}
STA adapts the LOO mutation paradigm~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey} to solver inputs; novelty lies in application and findings, not in the perturbation primitive.

